From e3fe5a034099bcced0ee5003ac2e47607a0f6f5a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ManicardiMatteo87 <manicardi.matteo87@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 17:42:34 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 16/19] perf(motion): fetch the G2 hot-path set from IRAM (P0,
 candidate G2)

QEMU flash trace of a86c680 (production Motion, rig, 5 596 releases of the five
P0 paths: WALK, WALK with IMU compensation, WALK STOP/SETTLE, STAND/HEIGHT with
two solves, RECOVERY) streamed into the 16 KB 8-way I-cache model of G1. With
G1 already in IRAM a release still touches 24-36 KB of flash code and misses
762-1 334 lines on average. Nineteen more functions, chosen by misses avoided
per byte (rank over the five paths, exact replay of the set): mean misses -34 %
(WALK), -34 % (compensation), -36 % (WALK STOP), -71 % (STAND/HEIGHT), -39 %
(RECOVERY); 9 966 B of text in the rig ELF.

- Motion library: contactInverseKinematicsGroundNormal, worldContactInverse-
  KinematicsTilted, validBodyPose, pointToBase, bodyPoseFromRollPitch, invert3,
  gaitOffset, legPhase, validGaitParameters, tiltFromQuaternion, the contact
  status convert.
- SimpleMotion: compensationTarget, readTimed, frameTimed, pointSafe, send,
  rememberReference, rateFromHistory, measured.
- Not placed: StopPlan::build and jointSlot (inline in headers: the l32r
  literal pool of a COMDAT function stays in flash, "literal placed after use"
  at link). StopPlan::build alone is another -7 points on the walking paths if
  it is ever defined out of line.
- Same machine code and results, a different section; no statement, value or
  signature changes. Each IRAM byte is a byte less of internal heap.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
